# British English's main volume. A word here is matched as the whole token it
# is written as, capitals and all, and a word in small letters is found for
# the same word with a capital at the front, as at the start of a sentence.
# One a line, the word, a tab, and what is said; a line starting # is a
# remark. tools/module/builtindict.py reads it.

# Titles before a name, said as the Oxford and Cambridge dictionaries have
# them, since the abbreviation dictionary that would expand them is off and
# nobody reads them by their letters. Written with the stop, an entry takes
# the stop after the word with it, so that Mr. Darcy is not cut in two by a
# sentence's end; written without, it is Mr Darcy as British text writes
# him. Dr and St are left out: each stands for two words, doctor or drive
# and saint or street, and an entry cannot tell which
Mr	mister
Mr.	mister
Mrs	`[.1mI.0sXz]
Mrs.	`[.1mI.0sXz]
Ms	`[.1mIz]
Ms.	`[.1mIz]
